// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/proc/exec.vuma
// VWK Process/Thread Subsystem — sys_exec (replace Task's mm + entry/stack
// for a new program) (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the K4d-exec layer of the VWK kernel: the execve() syscall
// primitive. K4a (task.vuma) built the Task control block + ProcessTable +
// task_alloc / task_free; K4d's fork.vuma (sibling file) built sys_fork
// to spawn a child as a copy of the parent. THIS file builds sys_exec to
// REPLACE a Task's address space and entry point with those of a new
// program — the Unix execve() semantic of "keep the PID, replace the
// program".
//
// ── execve() semantics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// execve() is the Unix primitive that replaces the current process's
// executable image with a new one loaded from a file. The PID, PPID,
// file descriptor table (fds, modulo FD_CLOEXEC), and process
// relationships all stay the same; the address space (mm_root), the
// instruction pointer (rip / PC), and the stack pointer (rsp / SP) all
// get replaced with the new program's. In a real kernel:
//   * The ELF (or other format) loader reads the executable file,
//     parses its program headers, and creates a new mm_struct with the
//     new virtual-memory mappings (text segment RX, data RW, bss
//     zero-filled, stack grown downward from a fresh top).
//   * The new mm_struct's PGD (page global directory) physical address
//     is written to the task's mm_root.
//   * The task's saved register file is updated: rip = ELF e_entry,
//     rsp = stack-top, rbp = 0, rbx/r12-r15 = 0 (a clean register
//     state for the new program — same as task_init_for_switch in
//     K4c's switch.vuma does for a freshly-spawned task).
//   * The caller's saved register file (which would normally contain
//     the return address into the syscall path) is DISCARDED — exec
//     never returns to the caller; the next context_switch returns
//     into the new program's entry point.
//
// ── K4d stub implementation ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4d contract specifies the stub:
//   * sys_exec(tbl, task_idx, entry, stack) — set task's rip=entry,
//     rsp=stack, mm_root=new.
// In the stub, only the mm_root field of the ProcessTable is actually
// written — set to the `entry` parameter (the new program's entry
// point). This is still a stub (no real ELF loader): in a real kernel
// mm_root would be the new program's PGD physical address (the ELF
// loader's output), and `entry` would be stored in the per-arch Task's
// rip field. The K4d stub has no per-arch Task wiring, so it parks
// `entry` in mm_root as a less-stubby placeholder than a fixed
// sentinel — the value now reflects the caller's intent (the entry
// point of the new program) rather than a hardcoded magic number.
//
// The `stack` parameter is accepted but NOT stored: the ProcessTable
// layout (re-declared here byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma) does
// NOT include a saved-sp / rsp field — the saved-register file lives
// in a separate per-arch Task struct (K4c's switch.vuma flat-fields
// Task layout). The TrapFrame (K3a's 22-field x86_64 trap-entry save
// area) is conceptually a Task field (`regs: TrapFrame` in K4a's Task
// layout) but is NOT stored in the ProcessTable's parallel flat byte
// arrays (only the 9 top-level Task fields — pid/ppid/state/prio/
// vruntime/mm_root/fs_root/fds/next — have parallel arrays). K4f's
// sys_exec syscall handler will take BOTH a State<ProcessTable> (for
// the mm_root update) AND a State<Task> (for the rip/rsp update) and
// call sys_exec for the ProcessTable side + task_init_for_switch (K4c)
// for the per-arch Task side. THIS file's sys_exec is the
// ProcessTable side only — `stack` is documented here as "to be
// stored in the per-arch Task's rsp field by K4f"; K11's real sys_exec
// will replace this stub with a real ELF loader that writes the new
// mm's PGD physical address to mm_root and sets rip=entry / rsp=stack
// in one shot.
//
// ── ProcessTable re-declaration ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the
// ProcessTable layout byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma (and to K4b's
// scheduler.vuma and K4d's fork.vuma). The byte-identical-redeclaration
// invariant (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay byte-identical" — extended
// to layouts in K3d) extends to ProcessTable: this file's pt_get_mm_root
// / pt_set_mm_root MUST see the same field offsets, field names, and
// field types as K4a's task.vuma. ProcessTable has 10 fields (9 parallel
// flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340 bytes — same as K4a.
//
// This file re-declares the 6 ProcessTable helpers it needs:
//   pt_get_state / pt_set_state     (u8 direct-index — needed by task_alloc)
//   pt_get_pid  / pt_set_pid        (u32 4-byte LE pack/unpack — needed by
//                                     task_alloc + the self-test's PID
//                                     invariant check)
//   pt_get_mm_root / pt_set_mm_root (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack — the only
//                                     ProcessTable field sys_exec writes)
// These 6 helpers are byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma. The other 12
// helpers (ppid get/set, prio get/set, vruntime get/set, fs_root get/set,
// fds get/set, next get/set) are NOT re-declared here — sys_exec
// doesn't touch them (exec preserves ppid/fds; prio/vruntime/next are
// scheduler concerns; fs_root would be replaced by the new program's
// VFS root in a real kernel, but the K4d stub leaves it alone).
//
// ── Why init-style? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4d contract's main() allocates `tbl = state_new(ProcessTable)` and
// passes it by reference to pt_init / task_alloc / sys_exec. This is the
// canonical PMT pattern (K2a's pmm_init(pool, pmm, ...), K3d's
// trap_frame_init(tf), K4a's pt_init(tbl) — see K2a's pmm.vuma header
// "Why init-style?" for the codegen rationale: the current codegen does
// NOT propagate State-typedness through function return values, so
// `fn exec_new() -> State<ProcessTable>` would produce broken field-
// access code in callers). All exec.vuma functions take
// `tbl: State<ProcessTable>` by regular VUMA-fn borrow (NOT extern
// #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need the attribute; only extern "C"
// calls do, and none of these functions call any extern).
//
// State<T> params: per the K4b contract's "Keep State<T> params to 1-2
// per function" rule (continued here), sys_exec takes 1 State<ProcessTable>
// param + 1 u32 (task_idx) + 2 u64s (entry, stack). Same discipline as
// K4a's task_alloc(tbl) (1 State param) and K4b's sched_tick(tbl,
// task_idx, delta) (1 State param + u32 + u64). The 2 u64 args (entry,
// stack) are passed by value — no State<T> needed because they're plain
// integers, not arena-backed buffers.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// ProcessTable is a flat-byte-array struct held in a `___pmt_buffer`-
// backed State<ProcessTable>; the CALLER allocates it via
// `state_new(ProcessTable)` and passes it by reference to pt_init /
// task_alloc / sys_exec / all pt_get_* / pt_set_* helpers (init-style
// API — see "Why init-style?" above). No externs are declared in this
// file (exec is pure-PM on the ProcessTable side — K4c's context-switch
// externs are what make the new program actually start running; THIS
// file just updates the ProcessTable's mm_root).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K4d contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14
// and rip=4096). The constants:
//     256  = number of Task slots in the ProcessTable (and the
//            "table full" sentinel returned by task_alloc)
//     4    = bytes per u32 field (loop bound for pid pack/unpack)
//     8    = bytes per u64 field (loop bound for mm_root pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     2    = READY state value (set by task_alloc on the new slot)
//     0    = FREE state value (checked by task_alloc's free-slot scan)
//     1    = PID offset (PID = slot + 1, so slot 0 → PID 1)
//   Self-test constants: 1000, 4096, 8192, 1, 2 — all decimal.
//   (4096 and 8192 are the entry/stack test values — 4096 = 0x1000 is
//   the conventional first-page-aligned entry point, 8192 = 0x2000 is
//   the next page; same values K4c's switch.vuma self-test uses for
//   prev.rip / next.rip, expressed in decimal per the same rule.
//   1000 is the initial mm_root test value (simulating the parent
//   program's PGD address); 4096 is also the post-exec mm_root
//   expected value, since sys_exec now stores `entry` as mm_root.)
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares ProcessTable + the 6
// pt_get_* / pt_set_* helpers + pt_init + task_alloc byte-identically
// to K4a's task.vuma (same pattern as K4b's scheduler.vuma re-declaring
// ProcessTable + 6 helpers + (no pt_init/task_alloc — K4b's self-test
// uses pt_set_state directly), K4d's fork.vuma re-declaring ProcessTable
// + 12 helpers + pt_init + task_alloc, and K3d's irq.vuma re-declaring
// irq_mask/irq_unmask/pic_eoi from K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma). K4d's
// exit.vuma will do a similar subset for its own needs.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/proc/exec.vuma \
//         /tmp/exec.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/exec.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (all 5 self-test checks pass — task_alloc
// returns slot 0 with PID 1, initial mm_root=1000 set, sys_exec
// replaces mm_root with 4096 (the `entry` parameter, no longer the
// 0xDEAD sentinel), PID stays 1 (exec preserves PID), state stays
// READY (exec doesn't change task state)).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ProcessTable: re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. 10
// fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340
// bytes. See K4a's task.vuma header "Why parallel flat byte arrays?"
// for the rationale (VUMA has no struct-array support).
layout ProcessTable = {
    pids: [u8; 1024],
    ppids: [u8; 1024],
    states: [u8; 256],
    prios: [u8; 256],
    vruntimes: [u8; 2048],
    mm_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fs_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fds: [u8; 2048],
    nexts: [u8; 2048],
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProcessTable helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6 of K4a's 18 helpers, byte-identical to task.vuma. exec needs:
//   state (u8 direct-index)    — for task_alloc's free-slot scan + READY write
//   pid  (u32 4-byte LE)       — for task_alloc's PID = slot+1 + the self-
//                                test's PID-invariant check (exec preserves
//                                PID — same process, new program)
//   mm_root (u64 8-byte LE)    — the only ProcessTable field sys_exec writes
// The other 12 helpers (ppid/prio/vruntime/fs_root/fds/next get/set) are
// NOT needed here — exec preserves ppid/fds (Unix execve keeps file
// descriptors minus FD_CLOEXEC ones, keeps the parent/parent relation-
// ship), and prio/vruntime/next are scheduler concerns (K4b owns those).

// Read the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_get_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.states[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.states[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u32 `pid` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.pids.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma (4-byte little-endian
// unpack loop — same pattern as K3e's syscall_table_get but with a 4-byte
// loop bound for u32).
fn pt_get_pid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u32 {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let v: u32 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.pids[off + i] as u32;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u32 `pid` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.pids.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_pid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u32) {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.pids[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `mm_root` field of Task slot `idx` from
// ProcessTable.mm_roots. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma
// (8-byte little-endian unpack loop — same pattern as K2a's pool_get_base
// and K3e's syscall_table_get).
fn pt_get_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.mm_roots[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `mm_root` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.mm_roots. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
// sys_exec calls this to overwrite the task's existing mm_root with the
// `entry` parameter (the new program's entry point) — still a stub (no
// real ELF loader); in a real kernel this would be the new program's
// PGD physical address (the ELF loader's output) and `entry` would be
// stored in the per-arch Task's rip field instead.
fn pt_set_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.mm_roots[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Lifecycle primitives (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pt_init + task_alloc, byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma. exec needs
// pt_init to zero the ProcessTable's states array before the self-test's
// initial task_alloc; exec needs task_alloc to spawn the Task that will
// be exec'd.

// pt_init: zero all 256 states (mark every slot FREE) and set count = 0.
// Idempotent. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_init(tbl: State<ProcessTable>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        pt_set_state(tbl, i, 0);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    tbl.count = 0;
}

// task_alloc: find the first FREE slot (state == 0), mark it READY
// (state = 2), assign PID = slot + 1 (so PID 0 is never handed out),
// bump count, return the slot index. Returns 256 if the table is full.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn task_alloc(tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        if pt_get_state(tbl, i) == 0 {
            pt_set_state(tbl, i, 2);  // READY
            pt_set_pid(tbl, i, i + 1);  // PID = slot + 1
            tbl.count = tbl.count + 1;
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 256;  // table full
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_exec — the execve() syscall primitive (ProcessTable side).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_exec(tbl, task_idx, entry, stack) — replaces the Task's mm_root
// with the `entry` parameter (the new program's entry point) and
// documents the `stack` parameter (which is NOT stored here — see
// below). This is still a stub: there is no real ELF loader, no new
// mm_struct, no PGD allocation. But the stub no longer uses the
// 0xDEAD sentinel — it stores the actual entry point as mm_root, so
// the value reflects the caller's intent (the new program's entry)
// rather than a hardcoded magic number. In a real kernel, mm_root
// would be the new program's PGD physical address; `entry` would go
// in the per-arch Task's rip field, and `stack` would go in the
// per-arch Task's rsp field (set via K4c's task_init_for_switch).
//
// The `stack` parameter is accepted (the contract's signature requires
// it) but NOT stored in the ProcessTable — the layout doesn't include
// a saved-sp / rsp field (rip / rsp live in K4c's per-arch Task
// struct). K4f's syscall handler will store `stack` via K4c's
// task_init_for_switch on the per-arch Task side; this function only
// updates the ProcessTable side.
//
// The task's other ProcessTable fields are preserved:
//   pid  — unchanged (exec keeps the PID — same process, new program)
//   ppid — unchanged (exec keeps the parent)
//   state — unchanged (exec keeps the task runnable — the caller is
//           already on the runqueue when it calls exec; the new program
//           inherits the runqueue slot)
//   prio — unchanged (exec keeps the priority)
//   vruntime — unchanged (exec keeps the CPU-time accounting — Linux's
//              execve also preserves vruntime, on the theory that the
//              process's CPU-time history is still meaningful even
//              though the program changed)
//   fs_root — unchanged in the K4d stub (a real kernel would update
//             fs_root to the new program's chroot context if applicable,
//             but K5's VFS isn't wired yet)
//   fds   — unchanged (exec keeps the file descriptors, minus any with
//            the FD_CLOEXEC flag set — that flag's handling is K5's
//            VFS concern)
//   next  — unchanged (the runqueue link stays as it was)
//
// In a real kernel, sys_exec would also:
//   * Free the old mm_struct (the parent program's address space) and
//     its page tables — K4d's stub does NOT do this (it just overwrites
//     the mm_root pointer, leaking the old PGD's memory; K5's VFS will
//     wire real mm lifecycle management).
//   * Parse the new executable's ELF program headers, create new VMAs
//     for the text/data/bss/stack segments, and write the new PGD's
//     physical address to mm_root — K4d's stub writes `entry` as a
//     placeholder (still a stub, but no longer the 0xDEAD sentinel).
//   * Zero the bss segment and push the argv/envp arrays onto the new
//     stack — K4d's stub doesn't (the new stack top is the `stack`
//     parameter, but it's not stored anywhere because the ProcessTable
//     doesn't have a stack field; K4f will store it via the per-arch
//     Task's rsp field).
//   * Set rip = ELF e_entry — K4d's stub doesn't (same reason as
//     stack — the ProcessTable doesn't have a rip field; K4f will
//     store it via the per-arch Task's rip field).
//
// The function is a transform returning the mutated ProcessTable
// (linear ownership semantics: the State<ProcessTable> is consumed by
// the transform and returned to the caller). execve either succeeds
// (in which case control never returns to the caller — the next
// context_switch jumps to the new program's entry) or fails (in which
// case the syscall handler returns -errno before calling this
// function). The K4d stub can't fail (it just overwrites one u64), so
// it doesn't have a failure path. K4f's syscall handler will check
// for ELF parse failures BEFORE calling sys_exec (so sys_exec is only
// called on the success path).
transform sys_exec(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, entry: u64, stack: u64) -> State<ProcessTable> {
    // Overwrite the task's mm_root with the `entry` parameter — the
    // new program's entry point. This is still a stub (no real ELF
    // loader), but no longer uses the 0xDEAD sentinel: in a real
    // kernel, this slot would hold the new program's PGD physical
    // address (the ELF loader's output), and `entry` would be stored
    // in the per-arch Task's rip field instead. Parking `entry` here
    // is the less-stubby placeholder — the value now reflects the
    // caller's intent rather than a hardcoded magic number.
    pt_set_mm_root(tbl, task_idx, entry);

    // The `stack` parameter is accepted (the contract's signature
    // requires it) but NOT stored in the ProcessTable — the layout
    // doesn't include a saved-sp / rsp field (rip / rsp live in K4c's
    // per-arch Task struct, set via K4c's task_init_for_switch). K4f's
    // syscall handler will store `stack` via task_init_for_switch on
    // the per-arch Task side; this function only updates the
    // ProcessTable side. The parameter is intentionally kept in the
    // signature (rather than dropped) so that K4f can pass it through
    // unchanged when wiring the per-arch side — and so the call site
    // compiles against the contract's documented signature today.
    //
    // Note: per the VUMA calling convention, unused parameters are
    // silently dropped (no warning, no error) — there is no leading
    // underscore because the parameter is now part of the function's
    // documented contract (it is the stack top the caller wants the
    // new program to start with, even if this stub doesn't store it).

    // Return the mutated ProcessTable — the transform "produces" it
    // (linear ownership semantics: the State<ProcessTable> is consumed
    // by the transform and returned to the caller).
    return tbl;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the K4d-exec DoD).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a ProcessTable via state_new, init it via pt_init, then:
//   (1) task_alloc → returns 0 (the only task in the table).
//   (2) pt_get_pid(0) == 1 (PID = slot 0 + 1).
//   (3) Set the task's initial mm_root = 1000 (simulating the parent
//       program's PGD address). Verify pt_get_mm_root(0) == 1000
//       (the initial value round-trips through pt_set_mm_root /
//       pt_get_mm_root).
//   (4) sys_exec(tbl, 0, 4096, 8192) — exec the task with entry=4096
//       (0x1000, conventional first-page-aligned entry point) and
//       stack=8192 (0x2000, next page). Should overwrite mm_root with
//       4096 (the `entry` parameter — no longer the 0xDEAD sentinel).
//   (5) pt_get_mm_root(0) == 4096 (exec replaced the mm_root with
//       the `entry` value; stack=8192 is documented-only, not stored
//       in the ProcessTable — K4f will wire it via task_init_for_switch).
//   (6) pt_get_pid(0) == 1 (exec preserves PID — same process, new
//       program).
//   (7) pt_get_state(0) == 2 (exec preserves READY state — the task
//       stays runnable; the new program inherits the runqueue slot).
// Returns 0 on success, 1..7 on the corresponding check failure (so a
// future CI failure pinpoints the broken check).
//
// Per the K4d contract's "decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (1000, 4096, 8192, 1, 2)
// — decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix
// width-extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma
// header for the same rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for
// the same rule applied to vector=14 and rip=4096). The values
// 4096/8192 are the K4c switch.vuma self-test's entry/stack values
// (0x1000/0x2000) expressed in decimal — same convention, so a
// future bare-metal run that wires K4c's task_init_for_switch to
// sys_exec can reuse the same test values.

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tbl = state_new(ProcessTable);
    pt_init(tbl);

    // (1) Alloc a task — should return slot 0.
    let task_idx = task_alloc(tbl);
    if task_idx != 0 { return 1; }

    // (2) The new task's PID should be 1 (slot + 1).
    if pt_get_pid(tbl, 0) != 1 { return 2; }

    // (3) Set the task's initial mm_root = 1000 (simulating the parent
    // program's PGD address). Verify the round-trip.
    pt_set_mm_root(tbl, 0, 1000);
    if pt_get_mm_root(tbl, 0) != 1000 { return 3; }

    // (4) sys_exec with entry=4096, stack=8192. The stub should overwrite
    // mm_root with 4096 (the `entry` parameter — no longer the 0xDEAD
    // sentinel). stack=8192 is documented-only (the ProcessTable has no
    // sp field; K4c's per-arch Task holds rsp) — the self-test just
    // verifies the call site compiles + the function runs without
    // crashing.
    sys_exec(tbl, 0, 4096, 8192);

    // (5) After sys_exec, mm_root should be 4096 — the `entry` value
    // (no longer the 0xDEAD sentinel). Verifies that sys_exec stores
    // the entry parameter instead of discarding it.
    if pt_get_mm_root(tbl, 0) != 4096 { return 4; }

    // (6) exec preserves PID — the task is still PID 1 (same process,
    // new program).
    if pt_get_pid(tbl, 0) != 1 { return 5; }

    // (7) exec preserves READY state — the task is still runnable
    // (state == 2, set by task_alloc; sys_exec doesn't touch state).
    if pt_get_state(tbl, 0) != 2 { return 6; }

    return 0;
}
